WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
Who really fathered a chick, or passed an extra chromosome to a child?

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.840
Watching birds feed a chick cannot prove genetic parentage.

3
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.220
Counting chromosomes alone misses which parent or division erred.

4
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.720
DNA markers answer both.

5
00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:24.343
A locus is a place on a chromosome, and its versions are alleles.

6
00:00:25.503 --> 00:00:31.063
At microsatellite loci, short repeated stretches of DNA vary between individuals.

7
00:00:32.543 --> 00:00:37.703
When parents carry distinguishable alleles at a locus, it forms an informative DNA

8
00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:43.843
marker. Suppose a father carries alleles 1 and 2, and a mother carries 3 and 4.

9
00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:49.443
Now put that marker tightly linked to the chromosome 21 centromere.

10
00:00:50.623 --> 00:00:56.423
Before division, each paired chromosome, or homologue, duplicates into sister copies

11
00:00:56.423 --> 00:00:57.643
joined at the centromere.

12
00:00:58.883 --> 00:01:03.623
Near the centromere, homologues can carry different alleles, while sister copies

13
00:01:03.623 --> 00:01:09.943
usually match. Distinguishable DNA alleles trace genetic parentage and each

14
00:01:09.943 --> 00:01:11.043
inherited chromosome.

15
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:18.070
Test this rule on Child A. The father carries alleles 1 and 2, and the mother

16
00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:23.250
carries 3 and 4. Child A inherits alleles 1, 3, and 4.

17
00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.750
Which parent supplied the extra chromosome, and which classical division failed?

18
00:01:34.383 --> 00:01:35.963
Trace each allele back.

19
00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:41.403
Allele 1 comes from the father, while alleles 3 and 4 come from the mother.

20
00:01:42.703 --> 00:01:44.763
So the mother supplied the extra chromosome.

21
00:01:46.303 --> 00:01:49.863
Meiosis I parts homologues, and meiosis II parts sisters.

22
00:01:51.303 --> 00:01:56.603
Receiving both 3 and 4 means the mother's homologues failed to part, a meiosis I

23
00:01:56.603 --> 00:01:57.603
error. Allele 1 comes from the father, while alleles 3 and 4 come from the mother.

24
00:01:57.983 --> 00:02:00.383
One condition can mislead this test.

25
00:02:01.603 --> 00:02:06.383
A crossover exchanges segments between two non-sister chromatids in meiosis.

26
00:02:07.863 --> 00:02:12.103
Between marker and centromere, that exchange can make sister copies differ.

27
00:02:13.423 --> 00:02:17.003
Then meiosis II can resemble meiosis I, and the reverse.

28
00:02:18.463 --> 00:02:21.083
Markers close to the centromere reduce that risk.

29
00:02:22.343 --> 00:02:26.763
Human eggs also make error patterns beyond the classical two-step model.

30
00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:32.153
DNA markers also tested wild parentage on Daphne Major.

31
00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:39.733
In 2001, Keller, Grant, Grant and Petren typed birds at eight microsatellite loci.

32
00:02:41.013 --> 00:02:44.393
Twenty percent of offspring in their sample were extra-pair young.

33
00:02:45.693 --> 00:02:48.233
Their social father was not the genetic father.

34
00:02:49.593 --> 00:02:54.313
In this study, pairing chicks with fathers who were not theirs made traits look less

35
00:02:54.313 --> 00:02:55.473
inherited than they are.

36
00:02:56.467 --> 00:03:01.447
Even after DNA checked parentage, chicks still resembled their mothers more than

37
00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:07.587
their fathers. The authors read that pattern as maternal effects, influences beyond

38
00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:09.747
genes like egg size or food.

39
00:03:11.007 --> 00:03:15.267
A maternal effect tracking the mother's trait can make inheritance look stronger

40
00:03:15.267 --> 00:03:16.267
than it is.

41
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:22.840
Alongside wild parentage, centromere-linked markers track extra copies of chromosome

42
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:29.300
21. They identify which parent supplied the extra chromosome and which classical

43
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:35.720
division failed. Wherever parental alleles differ, reading those DNA tags reveals

44
00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.940
who passed each chromosome copy.
